risti is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Corpus Christi's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Corpus Christi has set <strong>66 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 70 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">202 record highs</strong>, far more than the 70 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (80). Corpus Christi's all-time high is 109\u00b0F, on September 5, 2000.","t_chg":"Corpus Christi's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 138 complete years: Corpus Christi's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.16\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.21 to \u22120.10), about \u22122.2\u00b0F since 1888. Corpus Christi's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.60\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.55 to +0.66), about +8.2\u00b0F since 1888. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.57\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.28 to +0.85) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.76\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.42 to +1.10). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 8 months fell. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Corpus Christi has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Corpus Christi's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.54 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.18 to +0.89), about +7.3 inches since 1888. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 31.7 inches a year. The wettest year was 1888 (48.16 in); the driest, 1917 (5.38 in). Corpus Christi almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.3 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}